{"id": "tea:tattvartha-bhasya:1.1", "source": "src:tattvartha-bhasya", "location": {"ref": "1.1", "chapter": "1", "verse": "1", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Of the three [view, knowledge, conduct], when an earlier one is gained the later is optional, but when a later one is gained the earlier is necessarily present.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["karma-liberation"], "terms": ["trm:ratnatraya"], "concepts": ["cpt:three-jewels"], "teachers": ["tch:umasvati"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Recalled sentence of the bhāṣya on 1.1.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:tattvartha-bhasya:9.11", "source": "src:tattvartha-bhasya", "location": {"ref": "9.11"}, "paraphrase": "The Bhāṣya takes 'eleven in the Jina' literally: the eleven hardships arising from feeling-producing karma, among them hunger and thirst, do occur in the omniscient, who bears them.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "realized"}, "types": ["dispute", "powers-experiences"], "concepts": ["cpt:kevalibhukti"], "disputes": ["dsp:kevalin-eats"], "notes": "The Śvetāmbara reading (the Bhāṣya is held by Śvetāmbaras to be Umāsvāti's own); wording low confidence.", "reported_by_opponent": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U34-jain-canon"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U34-jain-canon"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:tattvartha-bhasya:prasasti", "source": "src:tattvartha-bhasya", "location": {"ref": "prasasti", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The closing verses name the author Umāsvāti, of the Uccairnāgara branch, disciple of Ghoṣanandi for initiation and of Mūla for study, son of Svāti and of Umā of the Vātsī clan, born at Nyagrodhikā, who composed the work at Kusumapura out of compassion for beings.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["teacher-transmission"], "concepts": ["cpt:jain-teacher-transmission"], "teachers": ["tch:umasvati"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Details recalled; to be checked (names of teachers and birthplace especially).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
